---
title: Yacla
description: Type-safe YAML and JSON configuration loading for Kotlin and Java applications.
---

**Yet Another Config Loading API** — Yacla loads YAML or JSON documents into Kotlin classes using their primary constructors. This documentation covers **4.2.0**, matching the current source version.

## What Yacla handles

- Copies bundled defaults when the target file is missing.
- Maps keys, nested objects, collections, enums, and Kotlin default values.
- Validates fields through annotations and reports conversion errors with field paths.
- Merges newer default documents while retaining user values.
- Selects localized default resources using the JVM language or an explicit language.

## Choose an entry point

| API | Use it for |
| --- | --- |
| `Yacla.yaml<T>()` / `Yacla.json<T>()` | Loading a config object directly |
| `yamlLoader<T>()` / `jsonLoader<T>()` | Keeping a loader for manual updates and reloads |
| `Yacla.loader<T>()` | Selecting parsers, update strategies, and language settings |
| `Yacla.withDefaults(...)` | Sharing defaults between several loaders |

## Documentation map

Start with [Getting Started](/yacla/getting-started/), then explore [mapping and validation](/yacla/mapping/), [config updates](/yacla/updates/), and [localized defaults](/yacla/localization/).

Java 17 or newer is required. Config objects currently need Kotlin primary constructors; Java applications can use the Java-friendly builder with Kotlin-defined config classes. Java records are not currently supported.

